---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-activecampaign';
const title = 'Drip vs ActiveCampaign for Shopify Email: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'Drip vs ActiveCampaign for Shopify — visual ecommerce workflows vs CRM automation depth, Shopify triggers, pricing, and team fit for retention operators.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Is Drip or ActiveCampaign better for a Shopify store doing $70k/mo?',
    answer: 'Revenue alone does not decide fit. Evaluate cart, post-purchase, and product-triggered branching for a DTC store; add CRM pipelines and lead scoring for wholesale or service upsells. Verify those capabilities in the current product, plan, and Shopify integration. Measure setup and edit time with the same proposed flows rather than promising faster revenue delivery.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Drip vs ActiveCampaign pricing compare at 12,000 contacts?',
    answer: 'Drip at 12k people typically lands $120–200/mo. ActiveCampaign Plus at 12k often runs $140–220/mo depending on feature tier. Similar bands — differentiate on workflow fit, not sticker price. Both punish list bloat; sunset stale contacts before comparing quotes.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which has the better workflow builder?',
    answer: 'Drip\'s canvas is clearer for ecommerce branching — cart value splits, product tags, order count. ActiveCampaign\'s automation builder is more powerful for CRM — deal stage changes, site tracking across non-Shopify pages, complex lead scoring. Choose Drip for DTC retention operators; ActiveCampaign for marketing-plus-sales automation.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can ActiveCampaign replace Drip for Shopify cart recovery?',
    answer: 'Evaluate replacement against the current integration and plan rather than assuming cart events or a launch deadline. Check event eligibility, purpose- and channel-specific permission, and current purchase, subscription, and suppression state before dispatch. Assign one sender and queue owner; cancel old queued recovery, prevent replay with stable deduplication keys, and verify rollback before enabling the replacement.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I also consider Sequenzy instead of Drip or ActiveCampaign?',
    answer: 'Sequenzy fits when lifecycle strategy speed beats canvas or CRM tinkering — guided playbooks with pay-per-email pricing. Drip wins visual workflow control for DTC. ActiveCampaign wins CRM automation breadth. Sequenzy helps teams who find Drip maintenance heavy and ActiveCampaign overwhelming for pure ecommerce.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which handles B2B wholesale better?',
    answer: 'For a proposed meal-kit corporate gifting evaluation, test inquiry-to-order stages, sales handoffs, and lead scoring in the current plan. For a coffee subscription evaluation, test recurring-order branches and subscription-state changes. These are proposed decision criteria, not documented merchant choices; confirm pipeline and integration support before selecting either product.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which integrates with Shopify more natively?',
    answer: 'Drip — built for ecommerce operators, product blocks, and order-triggered workflows are first-class. ActiveCampaign\'s Shopify integration is solid but feels like one connector among many — stronger when you also track WordPress blog engagement or non-Shopify lead sources.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What workflow test should decide the winner?',
    answer: 'Build welcome, cart, post-purchase, winback, and one CRM-triggered follow-up if applicable. Score time-to-live, Shopify trigger accuracy, and whether sales pipelines matter. Pure DTC lean Drip. Hybrid DTC-plus-B2B lean ActiveCampaign.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-cyan-100/90">/</span>
        <span class="text-cyan-100/90">Drip vs ActiveCampaign</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs ActiveCampaign for Shopify SMS</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">
          Visual ecommerce workflows versus CRM automation powerhouse. Drip optimizes for DTC operators who design behavior-based retention on a canvas tuned for orders, tags, and product properties. ActiveCampaign optimizes for teams who need deal pipelines, lead scoring, and automations that span Shopify plus sales inbox plus content site. Use three proposed evaluation scenarios: a $68k/mo coffee subscription, a B2B supplement wholesaler, and a home fitness brand with a content blog driving leads. These are planning examples, not observed tests or merchant results; confirm current product, plan, and integration scope before scoring either platform.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-cyan-100/90">This comparison weights SMS-native compliance, quiet hours, cost-per-message economics, and whether each platform treats texts as scarce interrupts — not email with fewer characters. Sequenzy is evaluated as the lifecycle email layer beside dedicated SMS senders.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'Shopify DTC', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Ecommerce canvas with order and product triggers.' },
            { label: 'CRM automation', tool: 'ActiveCampaign', reason: 'Pipelines, lead scoring, and sales follow-up.' },
            { label: 'Time to live', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Cart and welcome presets for pure ecommerce.' },
            { label: 'B2B + DTC hybrid', tool: 'ActiveCampaign', reason: 'Deal stages alongside marketing automation.' },
            { label: 'Also consider', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Guided playbooks if both feel like operator debt.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Drip</th><th>ActiveCampaign</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Shopify DTC workflow operators</td><td>CRM + marketing automation</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Starting price</td><td>From ~$39/mo</td><td>From ~$29/mo</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Billing model</td><td>People in account</td><td>Contacts + feature tier</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shopify depth</td><td>Native — ecommerce-first</td><td>Good — one of many connectors</td></tr>
            <tr><td>CRM pipelines</td><td>Limited</td><td>Core strength</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Workflow UI</td><td>Visual ecommerce canvas</td><td>Automation builder + CRM</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Lead scoring</td><td>Basic</td><td>Advanced</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Learning curve</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Moderate–steep</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Why this comparison matters for Shopify merchants</h2>
        <p>
          Drip and ActiveCampaign both offer visual automation beyond Mailchimp. The fork is whether your Shopify store is the entire business or one revenue line among CRM-tracked deals, blog leads, and sales follow-up. Drip assumes the operator thinks in orders and product tags. ActiveCampaign assumes marketing and sales share one contact graph with deal stages and site tracking.
        </p>
        <p>
          The coffee subscription chose Drip — 94% revenue from Shopify recurring orders, retention owner who enjoyed canvas logic for irregular reorder branches. The supplement wholesaler chose ActiveCampaign — 40% revenue from B2B inquiries needing pipeline stages from sample request to PO. Same price band, different operating systems.
        </p>

        <h2>Merchant scenario: home fitness blog plus Shopify</h2>
        <p>
          In a proposed $52k/mo home fitness scenario, assume 30% of email signups come from WordPress workout guides rather than Shopify popups. Test whether the current integration can distinguish three-article readers from popup discount seekers, with appropriate tracking permission. Compare native events and tag-based alternatives, including any custom integration labor; no cross-site capability or merchant outcome is established here.
        </p>
        <p>
          Time the same cart-recovery build in each evaluation environment and record operator experience, required integrations, and audit effort. Compare that with blog-to-buy nurture if it is the proposed growth constraint. No two-hour versus six-hour trial or merchant selection is verified here; prioritize the constraint demonstrated by your own evaluation.
        </p>

        <h2>Workflow test: welcome series</h2>
        <p>
          Propose a welcome test with popup, footer, and blog-referral branches. Evaluate whether current plan support allows lead-score thresholds so eligible high-intent readers can skip discount email two. Record build and review time rather than claiming an afternoon result. Use purpose- and channel-specific permission and current suppression checks; added branching is useful only if the scenario needs it.
        </p>

        <h2>Workflow test: abandoned cart</h2>
        <p>
          Propose an abandoned-cart test excluding wholesale-tagged customers from consumer promotions. Inspect branch visibility and audit effort in each current builder rather than claiming an observed winner. Recheck purchase, subscription, and suppression state at dispatch; designate one sender and queue owner, cancel stale queued messages, and deduplicate retries and replayed events. Compare attributed orders separately from causal lift: randomized eligible holdouts need uncertainty estimates and adequate samples.
        </p>

        <h2>Pricing reality at growing list sizes</h2>
        <p>
          At 18k contacts, both often land $180–300/mo. List hygiene matters equally — popup imports inflate bills. The coffee subscription sunset 4k stale leads quarterly; saved ~$40/mo on Drip. ActiveCampaign's sales seats add cost if CRM users need full licenses. Model total seats, not marketing contacts alone.
        </p>

        <h2>Team fit: who should choose which</h2>
        <p><strong>Choose Drip if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Shopify DTC is 85%+ of revenue</li>
          <li>Your retention owner thinks in workflows and product tags</li>
          <li>CRM pipelines are handled in HubSpot or not at all</li>
          <li>Cart, replenishment, and winback are primary KPIs</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Choose ActiveCampaign if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>B2B wholesale or service upsells need deal pipelines</li>
          <li>Blog, webinar, or non-Shopify leads feed the funnel</li>
          <li>Sales and marketing share contact records and automation</li>
          <li>Lead scoring drives nurture before first purchase</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Also consider Sequenzy</h2>
        <p>
          If Drip's canvas feels like maintenance debt and ActiveCampaign feels like CRM overkill for pure DTC, Sequenzy offers guided lifecycle playbooks with pay-per-email pricing. It will not replace ActiveCampaign pipelines or Drip's visual experiments. It replaces the strategic blank page for teams who know they need welcome and winback but not another builder to babysit.
        </p>

        <h2>Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          Drip wins Shopify-first DTC operators who want ecommerce canvas control. ActiveCampaign wins hybrid marketing-plus-sales automation with CRM depth. Run the five-flow test on both trials against your revenue shape. The winner is whichever platform your marketer trusts to edit the night before a flash sale.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/activecampaign-vs-klaviyo">ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-drip">Klaviyo vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-drip">Sequenzy vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/subscription-boxes">Email apps for subscription boxes</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/replenishment">Replenishment playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-12">
      <h2>TCPA, consent, and cost-per-message discipline</h2>
      <p>SMS on Shopify is not a faster email channel. Every send needs express written consent, documented opt-in source, quiet-hour configuration, and a margin model that includes platform fees, carrier surcharges, and discount depth. Postscript, Attentive, and Omnisend SMS expose compliance tooling — but merchants still own consent copy at checkout, popups, and keyword campaigns.</p>
      <p>Model cost per delivered message before scaling broadcasts. A recovered cart that required 25% off plus three texts is often negative contribution margin even when attribution dashboards look green. Pair urgent SMS with Sequenzy email when the message needs education, social proof, or multi-product context — never duplicate the same offer on both channels within twenty-four hours unless the subscriber opted into high-frequency updates.</p>
      <p>Weekly ops: audit STOP/HELP handling, reply inbox backlog, opt-out spikes by flow, and suppression overlap with email cart recovery. Quarterly: re-read popup and checkout consent language against live automations. TCPA litigation and carrier filtering both punish stores that treat SMS like promotional email with shorter copy.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>SMS operations depth 1: cost-per-message modeling including carrier…</h2>
      <p>For <strong>compare drip vs activecampaign</strong>, cost-per-message modeling including carrier fees and discount depth is not optional infrastructure. Shopify SMS apps that recover urgent revenue without wrecking consent treat every text as a scarce interrupt — cart recovery after meaningful checkout intent, launch access for opted-in VIPs, replenishment when SKU usage data supports timing.</p>
      <p>Postscript remains the SMS-native benchmark on this site: TCPA tooling, two-way replies, Shopify event triggers, and broadcast controls. Sequenzy is deliberately ranked as lifecycle email layer beside SMS — welcome depth, post-purchase education, winback arcs that should not be crammed into 160 characters. Klaviyo and Omnisend fit when one team wants bundled SMS with segmentation; Attentive when enterprise acquisition economics justify custom contracts.</p>
      <p>Run the math before scaling: platform fee plus carrier fee plus average discount per recovered order. A text that needs 25% off to convert may be negative contribution margin even when attribution dashboards look green. Pair channels deliberately — email for proof and education, SMS for timing — and never duplicate the same offer within twenty-four hours unless the subscriber opted into high-frequency updates.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Document opt-in source, message type promised, and maximum weekly frequency per segment.</li>
        <li>Configure quiet hours before enabling automations; audit checkout and popup copy quarterly.</li>
        <li>Assign one owner for reply inbox, suppression maps, and pre-launch TCPA review.</li>
        <li>Measure opt-out rate by flow, cost per incremental order, and full-price repeat purchase quality.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

